How long should a proposal document be?
Very simply put: as long as the relevant guidelines require!
The length of the proposal varies depending on the degree or unit level. For example, PhD candidates submit two different proposals: the first for admission, and the second for confirmation of candidature at the first-year review point.
Word counts listed below exclude references and any tables, figures, or appendices, and are intended to provide a general guide only:

UNE Special Topic / Reading / Research Units (HASS301, HASS505, HASS506): 500–750 words;
Honours and Coursework Masters applicants: 500–2,000 words;
Masters by Research: 1,500–3,000 words;
PhD application for admission: 2,000–4,000 words.

For students enrolling in undergraduate or postgraduate special topic, reading, or research units at UNE (such as HASS301, HASS505, or HASS506), consult the UNE HASS Special Topic, Reading and Research Units guide. For these units, a concise 500–750 word proposal serves as a formal learning and research agreement between you and your supervisor. It confirms the proposed scope, reading schedule, assessment structure, and feasibility, as well as establishing whether any human research ethics approvals will be required prior to enrolment approval.
A word of advice: do not start off on the wrong foot with the academic panel or your supervisor by submitting a document that is too long or too short (i.e. &gt; ±10%). Word limits evaluate your ability to write concisely and define a realistic project. If you exceed the limit or are overly brief, your application runs the risk of being returned for revision or ruled out entirely.
